Pooled literal labels in the listing
// am1 pools a bank's literals BY VALUE, so two references whose values
// coincide share ONE pool word. That is correct and deliberate: the word is
// the same 18 bits either way, and which bank an indirect through it reaches
// is decided by the extend-mode state at execution, not by the pool.
//
// Until 06-Sep-26 the listing rendered every reference sharing a slot from the
// pooled symbol's own expression tree -- whichever reference interned the slot
// first -- so the second reference here was annotated with the first one's
// symbol, bank qualifier included. In Adventure that printed a bank-0
// subroutine call under an unrelated bank-2 string label.
//
// The two references below are written differently but resolve to the same
// 12-bit value, so they must share one pool word AND still be labelled with
// what the source wrote. run_regression.sh checks both halves; a run where
// they stopped sharing the word would make the label check vacuous.
//
// Tools/AM1/Tests/litpool_bankcollide.am1 is the same case with the collision
// under a -D switch, for looking at by hand.

100/
    hlt

// alpha is a bank-0 address; beta is a bank-1 offset. Both are DEFINED before
// they are referenced, so both values are known when the pool slot is chosen
// (a forward reference gets its own slot and no sharing happens at all).
bank 0
2000/
alpha, 0

bank 1
2000/
beta, 0

3000/
    lac [alpha:0        // FAR reference  -- bank 0's 12-bit address
    lac [beta           // NEAR reference -- bank 1's own 12-bit offset
    hlt

constants

bank 0
constants
start 100
